<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Ley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Ley was a Nazi politician who headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a> from 1933 to 1945, after the trade unions were dissolved, and was the party's Reich organisation leader. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strength-through-joy">Strength Through Joy</a> leisure organisation. Indicted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a>, he hanged himself in his cell in October 1945 before the trial began.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-ley.jpg" alt="Robert Ley" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Article writer: Chris Trueman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Ley_(cropped)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Ley was born in Niederbreidenbach in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>, the seventh of eleven children of a farmer. He studied chemistry at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> and Münster. He volunteered for the army in 1914, served in artillery on both the eastern and western fronts, and in July 1917 was taken prisoner when his aircraft was shot down over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It has been suggested that he suffered a traumatic brain injury in the crash; for the rest of his life he spoke with a stammer and had bouts of erratic behaviour, worsened by heavy drinking.</p><p>Released in January 1920, he took a doctorate and worked as a food chemist for a branch of IG Farben. Enraged by the French occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, he became an ultra-nationalist and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> soon after reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s speech at his trial after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a>. He rejoined the re-founded party in March 1925 and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of the Southern Rhineland that July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ley edited a virulently anti-Semitic newspaper, the Westdeutscher Beobachter, from 1928 and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> from September 1930 until May 1945. Although sympathetic to the working-class wing around Gregor Strasser, he always sided with Hitler in party disputes. When Strasser resigned in December 1932, Hitler took over party organisation and made Ley his chief of staff.</p><p>On 10 May 1933, after the suppression of the trade unions, Hitler appointed him head of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a>, which became an arm of the state for deploying and disciplining labour, particularly for rearmament. In November 1933 it founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strength-through-joy">Strength Through Joy</a>, which offered subsidised holidays, cruises, concerts and sport and was among the regime's most popular programmes. The Front also took over the Volkswagen project; the first models appeared in February 1939, but none of the 340,000 workers who paid for a car received one. Ley became notorious for drunkenness and for embezzling the Front's funds.</p><p>After the outbreak of war in 1939 his influence declined, as Fritz Todt and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a> took over direction of the workforce, but he stayed in Hitler's inner circle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>American paratroopers captured Ley on 16 May 1945 in the village of Schleching, where he had given a false name. He was indicted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a> for conspiracy, war crimes and crimes against humanity. Three days after receiving the indictment, in October 1945, he killed himself in his cell, one of two of the 22 leaders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> to do so, the other being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>. The psychiatrist Douglas Kelley believed his behaviour came from organic brain damage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Ley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Independent organisations were replaced by Nazi bodies such as the German Labour Front under <b>Robert Ley</b>, the Hitler Youth and the Strength through Joy leisure organisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Niederbreidenbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Nuremberg Court Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.89,7.48&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Niederbreidenbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Robert Ley"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5403 5753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5403 5753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.89,7.48&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
